2.18.m:
Having performed purification in a pure place, facing east, fully composed, / or facing north, knower of dharma, and keeping his hands between his knees,
he should always sip water, knower of dharma, with washed feet and hands; / but not with water poured with one hand, or full of foam and bubbles,
nor indeed with water heated by fire should a wise man sip. / At the tips of the fingers is the place of the gods; at the base of the fingers is that of Brahmā;
at the base of the forefinger is that of the ancestors, and at the base of the little finger is that of men. / With the part sacred to Brahmā he should drink water three times, and then wipe his mouth.
He should next touch the bodily apertures with water, as well as the crown of the head and the navel. / Having sipped water, he should then chew the tooth-cleaning stick;
having chewed it, Rāma, he should always throw it away attentively, / facing north or facing east, never facing the southern direction.
He should not chew meat, nor a stick dry at the top, nor one that is slimy, / nor one that is hollow, sweet, sour, barkless, or with leaves attached;

biting the nails, and grinding the teeth, / and cutting hair with the nails—he should avoid useless actions;
and making sounds with mouth and limbs, or useless whistling and shouting. / Having wrapped his head, he should never sleep at night.
Likewise, O Rāma, he should not wander at night without a lamp; / he should not enter a cattle-shed by a non-door, nor an enemy's house by a non-door;
he should not enter or remain [there], and he should not awaken one who is asleep; / he should not interrupt another's speech, nor alienate one who is attached;
he should not break off a conversation, nor reverse his garments; / he should not stay long with raised knees, nor leap over a jumping [cow];
a cow grazing in another's field or suckling a calf / he should not report to another, nor should he point out a rainbow;
he should say, 'Good, good', and never utter what is unpleasant. / A seat made of palāśa wood is to be avoided, as are a footstool and sandals [of it].

2.19.m:
or of the mother's, O best of the descendants of Bhṛgu; and he should perform the śrauta rites in the consecrated fires. / Because a man is born in debt to the gods, / the ancestors, and the sages,
he should always strive for liberation from that [debt]. / A living being becomes free from debt to the gods through sacrifices, O giver of honor,
or, if he has little wealth, through worships, fasts, and vows; / and he becomes free from debt to the ancestors through the śrāddha and worship;
to the sages, through celibacy, sacred learning, and asceticism. / What is offered, what is not offered, what is cast out, what is well-offered,
and what is tasted, O fortunate one—he should not omit the five sacrifices. / One should know that what is offered in the fire is huta; what is the rite of bali-offering is ahuta;
the offering of rice-balls, O Rāma, is called nirvāpya; / and prahuta is according to the sacrifice, as is the ancestral sacrifice;
and prāśana is likewise declared to be that which is eaten immediately afterwards. / A householder should always perform the five sacrifices for the removal of sin:
the sacrifice to the gods, the sacrifice to beings, and likewise the sacrifice to the ancestors, / the human sacrifice, and the sacrifice to the sages—he should perform these constantly, without sloth.

2.21.m:
Thus ends the one hundred and third chapter, entitled 'Description of the Bath of Bṛhaspati', in the narrative of Puṣkara to Rāma, in the dialogue of Mārkaṇḍeya, in the second book of the auspicious Viṣṇudharmottara.
Rāma said: / Tell of another bath, O venerable one, which dispels misfortunes— / one which, performed just once, frees from sins.
Puṣkara said: / For those afflicted by consumption, those suffering from malarial fever, / those tormented by shadow-madness, those suffering from malarial fever,
those afflicted by the seizure of Vināyaka, and those whose minds are utterly deluded; / those whose learning does not succeed, whose crops do not grow,
whose family sinks into ruin, whose merchandise finds no sale; / the man who, even though strong, cannot win victory in battle;
those whose undertakings, though properly undertaken, fail again and again; / those who have quarrels with friends, kin, and relatives;
the woman whose fetus miscarries, or dies as soon as born; / whose menses waste away and no child is born;

The bath should be performed in autumn or spring for one who is healthy, O descendant of Bhṛgu; / but at all times for one who is afflicted, destroying planetary afflictions and diseases.
In that case, O twice-born, a brahmin should bathe [another brahmin] during the Sāvitra muhūrta; / he should bathe a kṣatriya during the Maitra and Vijaya muhūrtas;
he should bathe a vaiśya during the Paitāmaha muhūrta, and a śūdra during the remaining ones. / The fifth and seventh lunar days are best for the bathing of a brahmin;
the thirteenth and third are distinguished for a kṣatriya; / the second and tenth for a vaiśya, O giver of honor;
the sixth and fourteenth are for śūdras in the rite of bathing. / The eleventh, the twelfth, and the dark fourteenth
are celebrated for the rite of bathing of all castes. / Under the fixed asterisms, under the Vaiṣṇava asterism, Puṣya, Revatī, the asterism whose deity is Bhaga,
Hasta, Punarvasu, the Saumya asterism, Jyeṣṭhā, the Vāruṇa asterism, / and Dhaniṣṭhā—bathing is celebrated for all.
